{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Not one day shall you eat</b> — its meaning: not one day only shall you eat, and not two days only. And similar to it, <b>לא אחת ולא שתים</b> (II Kings 6:10) — meaning not one and not two only. And in my opinion, one who eats or does a thing for many consecutive days is not called one who eats or does for one day, and likewise one who eats for thirty consecutive days is not called one who eats for twenty days. Or the word \"one\" may be carried forward — not one day shall you eat, nor two, nor ten, nor twenty-odd days, but a full month. And behold, He gave them their desire in meat and not in fish and not in vegetables, because the essence of their request, about which they wept, was \"Give us meat\" (Numbers 11:13). And the sense of <b>a month of days, until it comes out of your nostrils and becomes loathsome to you</b> (Numbers 11:20) — that He would give them a great quantity of meat and they would eat from it the entire month in very great abundance, until they loathed it and it became to them like an abhorrent and foreign thing that is not eaten. And according to the plain sense, so it was: for on the first day they were struck a severe blow and the people who craved it died first — they are the rabble that was among them, and also the children of Israel mentioned first who said, \"Who will feed us meat?\" (Numbers 11:4). And the rest of all the people who wept afterward, each at the entrance of his tent (Numbers 11:10), ate from it a month of days, and it became loathsome to them, and they cast away the refuse that remained to them from it. And so the psalm says: \"And He rained upon them like dust, flesh... and they ate and were greatly sated, and their desire He brought to them. They did not lack their desire; while their food was still in their mouths, the wrath of Elohim ascended\" etc. (Psalms 78:27–31) — for some of them ate and were greatly sated, and those possessed of craving did not part from their craving at all, for while their food was still in their mouths the wrath of HaShem came upon them. And the midrash of Rabbi Shimon and of Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi does not fit the language of Scripture, because He said: \"Now you shall see whether My word comes to pass or not\" (Numbers 11:23). And the midrash of Rabbi Akiva, who said that the words mean what they sound like — \"Who is sufficient for them?\" — is the true meaning of Scripture, and this is the opinion of Onkelos. But the matter itself is astonishing, as Rabbi Shimon said: He about whom it is written, \"In all My house he is trusted\" (Numbers 12:7) — would he say, 'The place is not sufficient for them'?! And furthermore, they had all already seen wonders greater than this. And Rashi explains that Moses thought HaShem would not perform a new miracle but would only vindicate His prophet. And this too is not correct in my eyes, for He had already done something like this with the first quails (Exodus 16:13), and likewise with the water (ibid. 17:6) and with the manna (ibid. 16), for all of these were given to them in response to their complaints. And what seems correct to me is that when HaShem performs signs and wonders for Israel — they are kindness from Him, and all of them are for their good, for HaShem is good to all and His mercies are over all His works, except when wrath goes forth against those who transgress His will, that He deals with them in anger and in the attribute of judgment for complete evil. And behold, there is in miracles only complete good in mercy, or vengeance in the attribute of judgment. But now, when He told him that He would give them their request and they would eat meat until it came out of their nostrils and became loathsome to them, our master Moses knew that there would be no sign from HaShem to create meat for them as He had given them grain of heaven, in accordance with what the Sages say (Sanhedrin 59b): nothing impure descends from heaven. And furthermore, all the miracles He makes known to Moses beforehand: \"Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you\" (Exodus 16:4), \"Behold, I stand before you there upon the rock\" (ibid. 17:6). But here, where He said to him, \"And to the people you shall say: Sanctify yourselves for tomorrow,\" and did not inform him — Moses understood that this would not be through a miracle from Him, blessed be He. Therefore he asked in wonder: what can be done for them in the manner of all the earth? If one were to slaughter for them sheep and cattle from all their surroundings — it would not suffice for them, and if one were to gather for them all the fish of the sea that are in the place near them — it would not suffice for them. And HaShem answered him that the hand of HaShem is not shortened to grant them their request even by way of natural events. And this is the meaning of \"whether My word comes to pass or not.\" And He said, \"Is the hand of HaShem shortened?\" in the same idiom as \"royal wine was abundant according to the hand of the king\" (Esther 1:7). And HaShem did not say, \"Is anything too wondrous for Me?\" (הממני יפלא כל דבר), for He would not do this through a miracle. And so it was: a wind set forth from HaShem (Numbers 11:31) according to the custom of the world — not \"a very strong sea wind\" and not \"a fierce east wind\" as He mentions in the miracles (Exodus 10:19, ibid. 14:21), but an ordinary wind as is customary — \"and it drove quails from the sea,\" not that they were created now on their account, and there is nothing novel beyond the nature of the world. And furthermore, they had already experienced this before; nothing was innovated now except that the quails were very numerous."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:49.089651831Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}